[
{"claim": "COMMAND IDS ARE THE PEER-TO-PEER WIRE CONTRACT. The jsapi send_* wrappers resolve a command name to the CALLING instance's command id and ship the id to the remote (app.app.exec takes lib + id; the remote does Command::new(db, id) and panics 'Missing file data/...' when the id is absent). Signature compatibility is irrelevant if ids differ. Consequences: (1) NEVER delete_command + re-upsert a command that remote peers call - upsert_command alone already preserves BOTH the command record id and the rust source id for an existing name, so plain upsert is always the id-safe edit; delete-first is only for genuinely retiring a command; (2) dev.code.patch_command_body with an empty old_snippet is BROKEN - it ballooned a 1.8KB source record to 3.7MB of nested content (fold2, 2026-08-11) - use upsert_command for full-body rewrites; (3) save_command requires a live HTTP session (its app_read fails with Key 'data' not found over MCP). This id contract is what broke the owner's first v52 rollout against v51 peers, and retroactively explains the original v44-lineage 'incompatibility' too.", "tags": "platform-api,p2p,exec,ids,wire,upsert,patch_command_body,bug", "source": {"doc": "live v51 peer panic log from the owner + app.app.exec source + fold2/fold3 verification, 2026-08-11"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1786447774149},
{"claim": "The peer proxy cannot stream InputStream command results: peer.local (newbound_core) creates a peer stream (begin_stream/write_stream pump) ONLY for `file` responses - a bytes `body` (an InputStream return like camera live) is a process-local DataBytes ref that cannot cross the wire, so the viewer-side peer.remote finds neither stream_id nor usable body and returns its 404 sentinel. Symptom: remote live view falls back to JPEG polling instantly (File-returning commands - snapshot, last_keyframe, play - stream fine over peers, which is why everything else works in the HUD). Fix sketch (platform, owner blessing required per the boundary doctrine): a third branch in peer.local mirroring the file branch - when body is bytes, begin_stream and pump DataBytes.read into write_stream until close, carrying the mime type; peer.remote already join_streams. Found 2026-08-12 diagnosing the owner HUD report.", "tags": "peer,streaming,inputstream,proxy,live,platform-gap", "source": {"doc": "newbound_core/src/peer/peer/local.rs + remote.rs read, owner HUD report, 2026-08-12"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1786542476727},
{"claim": "InputStream-over-peers landed on newbound branch claude/peer-stream-bytes-bodies (2026-08-12, standalone off master, awaiting owner review): peer.peer.local gained a bytes-body branch (begin_stream + DataBytes pump, 16KB chunks, mime carried, 30s starvation cap) and peer.service.listen gained the s_4 close-back - s_2 detects a dead local reader (write false / entry gone) and answers s_4, whose handler retires the matching STREAMWRITERS entry so the senders write_stream fails and its pump stops ~1s after viewer disconnect. Wire-compatible with old peers in both directions. Two-instance lab verification: same-host peering works via discovery master/slave + setuser with keepalive (maintenance auto-connects; peer users must be in a group check_security accepts - production uses admin; a non-admin group 404s every proxied command instantly, which is also the diagnostic signature of an unauthorized peer). Remote status/file/live all green; live delivers ffprobe-clean fMP4 into an in-page Chromium fetch with correct avc1 codec parse. CRITICAL build gotcha: dev.code patches on a STATIC library regenerate src and build the rlib but do NOT relink the host binary - test runs silently exercise the old code until a cargo build relinks (~2s); check strings <binary> for a new literal before trusting a static-lib test.", "tags": "peer,streaming,s4,close-back,inputstream,lab,static-lib-relink", "source": {"doc": "two-disposable peer lab, newbound claude/peer-stream-bytes-bodies, 2026-08-12"}, "confidence": "low", "time": 1786546269385, "adjudicated": 1793532713964},
{"claim": "The popup machinery centers a dialog card at its open-time height and never re-centers, so content that fills in after open (e.g. the LAN discovery list) grows the card past the bottom of the viewport.", "detail": "Found fixing F2 (Add Connection clipping): dropping the fixed card height alone is not enough - the tab body needs its own scroll cap (calc(50vh + 40px) used) so late-arriving rows scroll instead of growing the card, and an empty tab needs a min-height close to the populated tab so the open-time measurement that drives centering is honest.", "tags": "peer.peer,frontend,popup,css,dialogs,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793392959847},
{"claim": "syncPeers guards app.scenehost's async addControl with me.adding, so each peer gets exactly one orb", "detail": "scenehost.addControl registers the key in byId/children only after its awaits, and syncPeers fires from both the waitReady and send_info callbacks, so unguarded overlap added every peer twice - and the duplicate stayed a frozen ghost because syncPeerData updates only the byId instance. Fix (F4, commit ad6575f9) lives entirely in peer.peer js: me.adding[id] marks the in-flight add; syncPeers skips a peer when byId(id) or me.adding[id] is set; the remove loop never removes a key still in me.adding; addPeerOrb's settle handler (resolve or reject) clears the flag and immediately removes the orb if its peer left while the add was in flight. app.scenehost is shared - fix callers, never the host.", "tags": "peer.peer,scenehost,orbs,race,F4", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "9068e44b817d47be"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793417921382},
{"claim": "peerErrorText(result) is the single wording for a failed peer call: 'Unauthorized' for an UNAUTHORIZED msg (prefix match), else 'Error: <msg>'", "detail": "F5 (commit 2f7e40a2). Every Update Peers row must end in one of three states - chips, removed (nothing to update), or an error - never 'pending...'. rowError paints the status cell with .rowerror (dim red) and disables the row's checkbox. The peer proxy suffixes the refused command ('UNAUTHORIZED: libs'), hence the prefix match. checkForUpdates fetches ../app/libs first when document.body.locallibraries hasn't landed; a refused dev/crate_versions just leaves the crates chip out. 'Unauthorized' is Alex's exact chosen word; the F10 update-flow rewrite (session 2) must keep calling peerErrorText.", "tags": "peer.peer,update-peers,errors,F5", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "9068e44b817d47be"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793417925088},
{"claim": "Untrusted strings on the Peers page go into the DOM with textContent and dataset, never innerHTML", "detail": "F3 (commit a3aa0206). Discovery names ride unauthenticated LAN UDP packets (port 5770), and peer display names, library ids/versions and error msgs come from remote peers. The LAN table rows, buildUpdateDialog's rows, the update/crates chips, and the #updateallpeersnow error lines are built with DOM calls + textContent; data-* carriers use dataset, never concatenated HTML. Static shells may stay literal innerHTML. Server side is still open: discovery.rs stores packet bytes 40+ as the name with no authentication - follow-up for Marc.", "tags": "peer.peer,xss,security,F3", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "9068e44b817d47be"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793417928260},
{"claim": "The Add Connection card (.addusercard) sizes to content and the TAB BODY scrolls - the card itself must not, because the popup machinery centers the card at its open-time height", "detail": "F2 (commit 2ec3bf86). The fixed 367px height is gone: .addusercard caps at calc(100vh - 80px), width 600px (calc(100vw - 32px) under 640px). .addusercard .tab-content gets overflow:auto with max-height calc(50vh + 40px): the cap stays near half the viewport so a LAN list that grows AFTER the open can never push the card's bottom off screen. .discovered keeps min-height:280px so the empty LAN tab measures near the Connect tab's height at open-time centering. .card is overflow:hidden by the shared kit - don't fight it, scroll the tab body.", "tags": "peer.peer,css,layout,add-connection,F2", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "css", "hash": "d45055d480860ed7"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793417934613},
{"claim": "buildUpdateDialog reads peer names from me.peers (the same objects as #peer_<id>.DATA), not from a json reply - stub a name in tests by mutating that live record", "detail": "Session-1 QA (H05): wrapping the page's json() helper cannot stub display names in the Update Peers dialog because no json call carries them; mutate document.getElementById('peer_'+id).DATA.name (shared with me.peers) instead. Also relevant for QA: agent.browser.act refuses to click <tr> rows (reports them disabled) - use the plain click tool for table rows.", "tags": "peer.peer,testing,qa,stubs", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "9068e44b817d47be"}, "confidence": "medium", "time": 1793417937428},
{"claim": "Peer discovery still stores unauthenticated UDP-5770 packet bytes as device names server-side (discovery.rs); only the client rendering was hardened.", "detail": "The F3 fix (peers-app-fixes, 2026-10) made the Peers UI render LAN-discovered names with textContent so injected strings are inert, but the server's discovery path accepts and records whatever bytes arrive on the UDP broadcast port with no validation or authentication. Any host on the LAN can plant arbitrary device-name strings in the discovery cache. Validating/authenticating discovery payloads is open, deferred to Marc at the session-1 hand-back.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.service,discovery,security,udp,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793418142452},
{"claim": "Automation opens a peer's HUD by calling the scene host's hostData.onFocus(<peer-uuid>) directly - the exact handler an orb tap fires - instead of attempting a WebGL canvas click.", "detail": "Used in peers-app-fixes Phase 0: orb hit-testing inside the WebGL canvas is impractical to drive from scripts, but app.scenehost exposes the focus handler on hostData, and invoking it with the target UUID exercises the same code path as a real tap, opening peer.headsup with the correct record.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,app.scenehost,qa,webgl,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793479277272},
{"claim": "A peer's last_contact cannot show real TCP recency: the server stamps last_contact = now for any live TCP link, and drops the field entirely for a disconnected peer.", "detail": "Confirmed during F6/F15 (peers-app-fixes MEDIUM phase 1): a connected TCP peer always renders full brightness and 3 freshness dots because every read sees last_contact = now, and a down peer shows 'last check unknown' because the field is absent - both are honest renderings of what the server records, not UI bugs. Making the dots reflect actual TCP check recency requires a backend change (flagged as a follow-up for Marc in the phase 5 report).", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,last_contact,recency,f6,f15,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793482251381},
{"claim": "initPopups reads a trigger's data-popup attribute at click time, so stripping or restoring the attribute enables/disables a popup menu item live, with no re-init.", "detail": "Used for F11 (peers-app-fixes): when the first controls_shared read fails with a non-NOT-FOUND error, the Add Control menu item's data-popup attribute is removed (plus a title explaining why), and the popup can never open - clicks do nothing because the attribute lookup happens per click, not at wiring time. Restoring the attribute re-arms it.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,popup,frontend,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793484080057},
{"claim": "document.body.peerupdater is the ONE shared library-update engine: peer.peer's js installs it, and both the Update Peers dialog and the headsup panel's Update All drive their installs through run(peerId, items, hooks) instead of their own chains (F10); document.body.peerAuthorWarn is the shared author-mismatch marker (F12).", "detail": "Contract (commit 88629f82, peers-app-fixes): items are {lib, version, chipEl} plus an optional {crates, chipEl}; hooks onStart/onDone/onError/onFinish. Installs run in chip order with the chip actually being installed marked; a failure reds ITS OWN chip with the error as textContent plus a Retry link and the chain continues; one run per peer (second start ignored), done-chips skipped on re-run; after the libraries a crates item replaces the recompile, else one compile_rust runs when any install answered data:true and its failure lands in the row summary. peerAuthorWarn: when both sides carry author+authorkey and they differ, the chip gets '⚠ different author', a title naming both, and starts dimmed/deselected until clicked in. Future edits to either update flow go through these helpers, never a re-grown per-control install chain.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,updater,f10,f12,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793485870442},
{"claim": "app.ui's .chip is a nowrap inline-flex row, so multiline chip content needs a block child with flex-basis:100% plus flex-wrap on the chip", "detail": "A <br> cannot break a flex container, and flex trims a text item's edge whitespace - which is why an error message and its Retry action ran together inside an update chip. The Phase 3b fix (commit b70a4fe9): put the error text in a block .chiperrline (flex-basis:100%), give the chip flex-wrap and auto height, and use margins (not whitespace) for gaps between inline items. Applies to any multiline content inside the shared chip style.", "tags": "app.ui,peer.peer,css,chip,flex,frontend,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793516462188},
{"claim": "data/peer carries stale non-live copies of the peers command source that still contain already-fixed bugs", "detail": "Records like irxu…cp4.rs, '…cp4 copy.rs', and x.rs sit beside the live impl (ywokvt…2l8) and still hold the publickkey typo after the F18b fix - a grep of data/peer for an old bug string hits this debris and can look like the fix never landed. They were deliberately left untouched during Phase 4.", "tags": "peer.peer,store,grep,gotcha,unreviewed", "confidence": "low", "time": 1793518287722, "adjudicated": 1793539179990, "superseded": {"time": 1793539179990, "by": "The stale peers-command copies in data/peer contain NO already-fixed bugs: their publickkey typo line was never a bug to fix, because publickey is deliberately kept in the peer record (Marc's F18b review, 2026-11-01); the copies are just old code.", "reason": "The new claim directly negates belief 0's assertion that the stale copies contain already-fixed bugs, explaining that the publickey line was never a bug because keeping publickey in the peer record was deliberate (Marc's F18b review, 2026-11-01)."}},
{"claim": "An instance's own UDP-5770 broadcast can land in its own discovery cache: the server-side own-UUID skip in peer/service/discovery.rs runs only when my_uuid is non-empty.", "detail": "Found on master during LOW F17 (2026-11-01): nb-peers-b's own broadcast appeared in its own LAN discovery list. The F17 fix filters self client-side in peer.peer's js, but the server-side gap remains and is a follow-up candidate for the hand-back to Marc.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.service,discovery,lan,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793525482954},
{"claim": "A programmatic element.click() opens a non-modal popup menu at the viewport's top-left corner: initPopups positions the me.popup card from the click event's clientX/clientY, and synthetic clicks carry (0,0).", "detail": "Diagnostic signature for 'menu opens in the corner' reports - it is the caller's coordinates, not a layout bug. QA automation must dispatch a MouseEvent with real clientX/clientY to exercise menu positioning; with real coordinates the menu opens beside the trigger and clamps on-screen. Also beware an armed once-close document handler from a prior open consuming the next synthetic click, yielding a 0x0 menu in screenshots.", "tags": "peer.peer,popup,initPopups,qa,automation,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793529078342},
{"claim": "UNFIXED (found 2026-11, handed to Marc): the non-modal popup's off-screen clamp ignores the popup card's 20px margin, so even a real click near the right viewport edge leaves the menu overhanging by ~10px (rect right 1450 at viewport width 1440).", "detail": "Same me.popup non-modal path as the clientX/clientY positioning. Found verifying peers-app-fixes LOW Phase 1b item 4; the corner-opening complaint itself was synthetic-click (0,0) coordinates, but this margin-blind clamp is a real residual bug in the shared popup machinery.", "tags": "peer.peer,popup,clamp,bug,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793529085886},
{"claim": "peer.peer renders through two paths: the 3D scene and a plain peer list (F18a). List mode is chosen by ?view=list, a failed WebGL probe, scenehost reporting headless, or a 5 s ready timeout, and it never mounts the scene or the feed.", "detail": "Commits 72965cd9 + 0a5fa7cb (peers-app-fixes). Rows are built from document.body.peerstatus (coloured dots, kind, last check) with textContent, inherit the app font via .peerlistwrap, refresh on the same 10 s re-read, and a row click opens the same peer.headsup panel an orb tap does. A dim note explains the fallback. Any future change to peer rendering, status wording, or the panel-open path must cover BOTH paths. QA: /peer/?view=list forces the list; in Noobscape, webgl.disabled must be removed from prefs.js with the browser closed to restore WebGL (removing user.js alone isn't enough). The headless and 5 s-timeout branches are code-reviewed only, because the WebGL probe fails first when WebGL is off.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,f18a,webgl,fallback,list", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "d182dd1ea232bfa4"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793532065963},
{"claim": "The Add Connection LAN tab curates discovery client-side: this instance's own UUID is dropped, loopback entries (127.0.0.1 / ::1 / localhost) carry a dim 'this machine' chip and sort after real LAN devices, and names/UUIDs ellipsize inside the card with title tooltips (F17).", "detail": "Commits 33ad124a + 6af60d76 (peers-app-fixes). Both the Add Connection and Update Peers cards anchor their close ✕ in the card header row, with the title on one line and the tabs below. The 'this machine' chip survives arbitrarily long names because the name ellipsizes in its own flex:1/min-width:0 span while the chip is flex:none — keep that split when touching the row layout. The self-entry exists because an instance's own UDP-5770 broadcast lands in its own discovery cache (the server-side own-UUID skip in peer/service/discovery.rs runs only when my_uuid is non-empty); the client filter is the only guard until that is fixed server-side.", "tags": "peer.peer,f17,lan,discovery,layout", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "d182dd1ea232bfa4"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793532070319},
{"claim": "data/peer carries stale non-live copies of the peers command source beside the live impl - just old code, not evidence about current behavior", "detail": "Records like irxu…cp4.rs, '…cp4 copy.rs', and x.rs sit beside the live impl (ywokvt…2l8), deliberately left untouched. A grep of data/peer can hit this debris and mislead: check the live impl id before concluding anything. Supersedes the earlier framing that the copies 'still contain already-fixed bugs' - their remove_property(\"publickkey\") typo line was never a bug to fix, since publickey belongs in the peer record (Marc's F18b review, 2026-11-01) and phase 6 reverted F18b's removal.", "tags": "peer.peer,store,grep,gotcha", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793536729119},
{"claim": "A peer's publickey is intentionally included in the records peer.peer.peers sends to the browser and must not be stripped", "detail": "Marc's review of F18b (2026-11-01): a peer's public key is public by design - everyone who wants to connect needs it - and only privatekey must never leave the machine. privatekey lives only in the local runtime (listen.rs / listen_udp.rs) and never appears in a user record, so user_to_peer strips password only. The original remove_property(\"publickkey\") was a typo that never removed anything, meaning publickey always reached the browser as intended; F18b's 9b8cecc3 briefly made the removal real and phase 6 of peers-app-fixes reverted it, leaving a comment in user_to_peer.", "tags": "peer.peer,publickey,security,f18b", "source": {"hash": "ad8fdb894a6f56f2", "repo": "newbound", "path": "newbound_core/src/peer/peer/peers.rs", "commit": "4438fdd7bb2c5dc3e8f5a24aa23c18ef8ed1f57b"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793536736805},
{"claim": "Orb tinting keys on the scene node id 'orb', not the geometry kind: swapping the peer_model record's shape (sphere to ico) leaves tint()/setRig and the freshness color pipeline working unchanged.", "detail": "Verified on the rig after merging master's peer_model record (kind \"ico\"): the live spec showed material.color #54c069 at freshness 1 over TCP, grey-and-dim (#6b6b6b, lightness 0.54->0.42) on peer death, and green again on reconnect. Closes 3-LOW.md's Status-section open question - as long as the node id stays 'orb', geometry swaps need no tint changes.", "tags": "peer.peer,scene,orb,tint,peer_model,ico,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793536863682},
{"claim": "Color assertions against the constellation's pooled InstancedMesh must expect linear-space encoding: a spec/material color of #54c069 reads back as instance color #178624.", "detail": "three.js stores per-instance colors in linear color space while the scene spec and material carry sRGB hex, so QA that samples instanceColor must convert (or compare against material.color instead) - a naive hex equality check fails on a correctly tinted orb. Measured during the peers-app-fixes Phase 5 smoke audit.", "tags": "peer.peer,scene,qa,instancedmesh,color,threejs,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793536870488},
{"claim": "Peer-link authentication is key-based, so resetting a peer user's password via setuser does not break peering.", "detail": "security.security.setuser requires a password on every call, so editing a peer user's groups replaces whatever throwaway password the original handshake stored. This is harmless: the encrypted peer channel authenticates by key exchange, not the user record's password, and the link reconnects normally afterward. Observed on the two-instance rig while toggling a peer between admin and a non-admin group.", "tags": "peer.peer,security.security,setuser,peering,auth,testing,unreviewed", "confidence": "medium", "time": 1793538657439},
{"claim": "The stale peers-command copies in data/peer contain NO already-fixed bugs: their publickkey typo line was never a bug to fix, because publickey is deliberately kept in the peer record (Marc's F18b review, 2026-11-01); the copies are just old code.", "detail": "Directly retires the earlier framing that the copies 'still contain already-fixed bugs'. The publickkey typo never removed anything, and removing publickey was itself the mistake (F18b, reverted in phase 6). The copies (irxu…cp4.rs, '…cp4 copy.rs', x.rs beside the live impl ywokvt…2l8) are deliberately untouched debris; check the live impl id before concluding anything from a grep hit.", "tags": "peer.peer,store,grep,gotcha,f18b", "source": {"doc": "Marc's F18b review + phase 6 revert, 2026-11-01"}, "confidence": "low", "time": 1793539179990},
{"claim": "Noobscape's eval runs content-scoped (no browsingContext, docShell or ChromeUtils), so window size and zoom cannot be driven from scripts: viewport changes go through the profile's xulstore.json with the browser closed, and a zoomed check is emulated by the equivalent CSS viewport (1920×1080 at 120% ≡ a 1600×988 window → 1600×900 viewport).", "detail": "Measured in peers-app-fixes Phase 7: window.browsingContext and window.docShell are undefined in the eval context and a //NOOBSCAPE-CHROME comment does not route eval to chrome (only the screenshot command uses the chrome channel). Layout at the equivalent CSS viewport is identical to the zoomed view; only rasterization differs - say so in the report. The title, Local Device card and F18a list share one 20px left gutter (equal getBoundingClientRect lefts is the audit).", "tags": "peer.peer,qa,noobscape,viewport,zoom,testing", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793540435204},
{"claim": "The headsup panel's width is a viewer setting: localStorage 'peer.headsup.width', clamped to [360px, 90vw], and every open path must go through hudWidth()/placeResizer in peer.peer js.", "detail": "Phase 7b (peers-app-fixes): #hudresizer is a col-resize strip that is a SIBLING of #headsupdisplay, not a child, because the panel scrolls (overflow-y) and an absolute child would ride the content up out of view - the js pins it at the panel's left edge (right = width - 3, straddling 3px each side). onSceneFocus opens by animating to hudWidth() (never a literal 680); drags use pointer capture so text never selects and the scene never sees a tap, and the hud watcher (watchHud) retires the strip when headsup's own ✕ closes the panel without telling peer.peer. List mode (F18a) shares the same open path, so the resizer works there for free. peer.headsup's .inline.big caps at max-width:100% so Dashboard tiles reflow at the 360px minimum.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,panel,resize,localStorage,7b", "source": {"lib": "peer", "ctl": "peer", "facet": "js", "hash": "ea0b5df00471829d"}, "confidence": "high", "time": 1793541909470},
{"claim": "document.body.peerReplyError(result, field) is the shared honest-success test for peer-proxied replies: it returns null only on real success, otherwise the failure text in peerErrorText's vocabulary — and peerErrorText now unwraps a nested {status:'err', msg} envelope buried in data or msg.", "detail": "Peer-proxied replies can carry an err envelope nested inside an ok wrapper, so the library updater previously counted failures as successes and rendered [object Object]. The updater now counts install_lib as updated only when peerReplyError(…,'data') is null AND result.data is a real boolean, and compile_rust only when msg is a real string. Landed in U1 on branch peers-update-honest (commit cf58cb1e).", "tags": "peer.peer,peerupdater,error-handling,updates,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793564512016},
{"claim": "app.ui's popup card clears its animated fixed height only in a WAAPI onfinish callback, which never fires while document.hidden is true — so on a locked/hidden QA screen the card stays at its small open-time height and overflow:hidden clips late-rendering content like dialog footers.", "detail": "Diagnosed in 4-UPDATES Step 7: the Update Peers dialog's footer button appeared missing in a before-screenshot (zero amber pixels below the clip line), but forcing the resting state onfinish would set on a live screen rendered it fully. A rig artifact, not a peer.peer defect: screenshots of popups taken on hidden documents can clip real content. Complements the known 'centers at open-time height, never re-centers' behavior.", "tags": "peer.peer,app.ui,popups,qa,screenshots,document-hidden,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793564525057},
{"claim": "document.body.peerPackage and document.body.peerPkgGate are the shared local-package reader and update-offer gate (U2): update chips promise only what THIS machine can actually install.", "detail": "peerPackage(lib, cb) makes one ../dev/lib_info call per lib, cached for the page's life and deduped across concurrent askers; null means no usable package (wrapped error, or version/hash missing). peerPkgGate marks a chip offerable only when the local package is NEWER than the peer's copy, and the chip then promises the PACKAGE's version (text and dataset.version), not the peer's self-report; anything else marks the chip nopkg - dimmed 0.45, 'not packaged here' note, excluded from clicks and both run paths (applyAuthorWarn guards too). checkForUpdates builds each dialog chip only after its verdict lands, so no chip can run unjudged. Run summaries append 'N not packaged on this machine'. Joins peerupdater/peerAuthorWarn as the page's shared engines.", "tags": "peer.peer,peer.headsup,updates,u2,lib_info,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793566309137},
{"claim": "Screenshots of an app.ui modal popup taken before its WAAPI onfinish show custom checkboxes as unchecked even when they are checked.", "detail": "The custom checkbox's amber :checked fill and the dialog card's responsive height only settle in the popup animation's onfinish handler, so a shot captured earlier paints the box empty. This resolved the 4-UPDATES Phase 2/Phase 3 contradiction over u2d-dialog-done: the Update Peers dialog never unchecks a row after a run - the 'unchecked' shot was this timing artifact, not state. App-side (app.ui popup machinery), not data/peer. For QA: wait for the popup animation to finish (or assert el.checked alongside the pixels) before trusting checkbox state in a screenshot.", "tags": "peer.peer,app.ui,popup,qa,screenshot,checkbox,unreviewed", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793610511508},
{"claim": "A Boolean/String/JSONObject command's caught panic arrives as status: ok with {status: err, msg} inside data/msg. Read replies with document.body.peerReplyError, never with status == 'ok' alone.", "detail": "Cause: format_result (newbound_core/src/app/service/init.rs) wraps a command's caught-panic err object like any other return value, so the outer envelope stays status:ok and the failure hides in data (Boolean/JSONObject commands) or msg (String commands). FLAT commands (e.g. dev/update_crates) arrive unwrapped as status:err, but route them through peerReplyError too, for safety. This is why Update All said 'All updated' while every install failed (session 4, U1, commit cf58cb1e on peers-update-honest). The server-side wrapping itself is Marc's to fix; pages must read replies honestly in the meantime. Companion claim: the peerReplyError(result, field) contract.", "tags": "peer.peer,format_result,wrapped-panic,error-handling,updates,u1", "confidence": "high", "time": 1793613921948},
{"end": true}]
